 record answers only when that key is absent. The hook answer is one of: the gate off; on with the unwired reviewer required; on with it warned; a mixed declaration; or an unreadable declaration, as IF-298 carries. The hooks no longer read a declared line themselves and instead reach it through that answer.\"\"\"\n test_refs = \"(see TC-031)\"\n status = \"Approved\"\n component = \"CMP-007\"\n@@ -1561,7 +1561,7 @@ sr_refs = [\"SR-137\", \"SR-139\"]\n title = \"The config layer: one parse, declaration checks, one comparison\"\n module = \"project-trajectory/scripts/agent_policy.py;project-trajectory/scripts/bootstrap.py\"\n code_symbol = \"process_config/config_conflicts/approval_through/human_holds;_migrate_dial_key_name\"\n-detail = \"\"\"The configuration layer parses the policy declaration once, reads a dial from that parse with its legacy declaration as fallback, and returns refusal text rather than raising for a duplicate declaration, a wrong-typed value or an out-of-range value. It supplies the ordinal approval level and the one comparison every consumer uses. Valid TOML syntax, including dotted keys, inline tables and multi-line strings, is not a refusal condition.\"\"\"\n+detail = \"\"\"The configuration layer parses the policy declaration once, reads a dial from that parse with its legacy declaration as fallback, and returns refusal text rather than raising for a duplicate declaration, a wrong-typed value or an out-of-range value. It supplies the ordinal approval level and the one comparison every consumer uses. Valid TOML syntax, including dotted keys, inline tables and multi-line strings, is not a refusal condition. A legacy dial key the migration cannot rename in place is refused rather than altered.\"\"\"\n rationale = \"\"\"The policy values serve several entry points, so their validation and comparison need one home. A duplicate or invalid declaration must surface as a refusal rather than letting each entry point choose a different fallback; valid TOML syntax is a parser concern, not a policy violation.\"\"\"\n test_refs = \"TC-150;TC-151\"\n status = \"Approved\"\n@@ -1572,8 +1572,8 @@ phase = 5\n sr_refs = [\"SR-138\"]\n title = \"The legacy converter, and the one word that is not a rename\"\n module = \"project-trajectory/scripts/bootstrap.py\"\n-code_symbol = \"migrate_legacy_config/_migrate_gate_policy/set_process_key/LEGACY_APPROVAL\"\n-detail = \"\"\"`set_process_key` answers THREE states (`wrote`/`unchanged`/`missing`) and the write decides the delete; `add_if_missing` makes the write total. `gate-policy` gets its own arm because its single word expands to three dials, so it cannot be a one-key `LEGACY_CONFIG` row. `LEGACY_APPROVAL` is F5-duplicated from `agent_common` (bootstrap imports no kit sibling — it must run standing on nothing but the kit directory it copies FROM) and pinned equal by `test_rule_sync`. Exposed as bootstrap.py --migrate-config, the pass both scaffolding and the documented re-sync run; the destination home is docs/process.toml.\"\"\"\n+code_symbol = \"PolicyWriteRefused;set_process_key;_toml_canon;_confirmed_edit;_locate_process_key;_unconfirmed_form;_refusal;_written;_report_flag;LEGACY_APPROVAL;_migrate_dial_ordinal;migrate_legacy_config;_migrate_legacy_file;_migrate_gate_policy\"\n+detail = \"\"\"`set_process_key` retains an in-place edit only when the TOML parse of the edited text equals the old parse with exactly that key set; every other candidate — including an inline table, dotted key, key-like line inside a multi-line string, or unparseable file — is refused and writes nothing. A scaffold flag ends with that refusal and prints its value only when written. A refused legacy migration keeps its legacy file; it deletes one only when the parse of the written policy declaration holds its value. `gate-policy` gets its own arm because its single word expands to three dials, so it cannot be a one-key `LEGACY_CONFIG` row. `LEGACY_APPROVAL` is F5-duplicated from `agent_common` (bootstrap imports no kit sibling — it must run standing on nothing but the kit directory it copies FROM) and pinned equal by `test_rule_sync`. Exposed as bootstrap.py --migrate-config, the pass both scaffolding and the documented re-sync run; the destination home is docs/process.toml.\"\"\"\n rationale = \"\"\"This is duplicated POLICY, not plumbing: a migrator and a reader disagreeing about what `single-approve` meant would let a repo scaffold with one posture and run with another — which is the shadowing defect the ordinal was introduced to remove, returning through the migration path.\"\"\"\n test_refs = \"TC-152\"\n status = \"Approved\"\ndiff --git a/docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.toml b/docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.toml\nindex c4fd57dbf..fd93d820e 100644\n--- a/docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.toml\n+++ b/docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.toml\n@@ -262,7 +262,7 @@ tags = [\"shell\"]\n need =\"\"\"The repo owner can find and change every policy dial in **one home** — a **single hand-edited, machine-read file** — and a repo that declares the same dial twice is REFUSED rather than resolved by precedence.\"\"\"\n why = \"\"\"The dials had accreted into ~10 one-word files under `docs/`, an idiom that was never ruled. Scattering costs an owner one file per question and a reader five ad-hoc parsers; worse, two sources for one dial can give enforcers different policy values, and a precedence rule makes that disagreement invisible instead of loud.\"\"\"\n priority = \"S\"\n-acceptance = \"\"\"A single declared process-dial file holds every process dial. Every reader obtains its policy answer from the file's TOML parse. A legacy one-word file still present alongside its key is a REFUSAL naming both, and a declared migration action — run by bootstrap and by the documented re-sync — converts and deletes the legacy files so an adopter never meets that refusal unaided. A wrong-typed or out-of-range dial is refused, never defaulted.\"\"\"\n+acceptance = \"\"\"A single declared process-dial file holds every process dial. Every reader obtains its policy answer from the file's TOML parse. A legacy one-word file still present alongside its key is a REFUSAL naming both, and a declared migration action — run by bootstrap and by the documented re-sync — converts and deletes each legacy file it can confirm in the policy declaration and, when it cannot write one, leaves it intact with a named refusal and hand-edit path so an adopter never meets that refusal unaided. A wrong-typed or out-of-range dial is refused, never defaulted.\"\"\"\n \n [need.SN-029]\n status = \"Approved\"\ndiff --git a/docs/requirements/system-requirements.toml b/docs/requirements/system-requirements.toml\nindex 822354633..31d602cd8 100644\n--- a/docs/requirements/system-requirements.toml\n+++ b/docs/requirements/system-requirements.toml\n@@ -214,7 +214,7 @@ boundary_refs = [\"B-05\"]\n hat_refs = [\"CONSISTENCY\", \"SECURITY\"]\n requirement = \"Every delivered enforcer of a declared policy dial shall read the same value for that dial as every other enforcer — one parse, one answer, across the hooks, the loop and the checkers alike.\"\n rationale = \"Realizes SN-028's one readable policy home. A policy answer calculated more than once can diverge across enforcers, leaving the owner unable to know which value governs; one parse gives every enforcer the same answer. SR-137 owns whether a declaration is ambiguous or invalid, while this row owns agreement among the enforcers that consume a valid declaration.\"\n-acceptance_criteria = \"Every enforcer reads the same value for the same dial from one TOML parse, including a dotted key, an inline table and a multi-line string; none refuses any of those valid TOML forms for its shape. A privacy gate or reviewer declaration outside `[policies]` reads as gate on and reviewer required for every enforcer, and an unreadable declaration refuses at the hooks rather than reading a default.\"\n+acceptance_criteria = \"Every enforcer reads the same value for the same dial from one TOML parse, including a dotted key, an inline table and a multi-line string; none refuses any of those valid TOML forms for its shape. A privacy gate declaration outside the policy home's policy table reads gate on for every enforcer; a privacy reviewer declaration outside that table reads reviewer required for every enforcer, and an unreadable declaration refuses at the hooks rather than reading a default.\"\n priority = \"M\"\n verification = \"Test\"\n status = \"Approved\"\n@@ -486,8 +486,8 @@ sn_refs = [\"SN-028\"]\n boundary_refs = [\"B-01\", \"B-04\"]\n hat_refs = [\"CONSISTENCY\", \"SECURITY\"]\n requirement = \"The kit shall read every process policy dial from a single policy declaration home and refuse a duplicate declaration or a wrong-typed or out-of-range value rather than resolve it by precedence or default.\"\n-rationale = \"A single declaration home gives the owner one place to inspect and change each dial. A duplicate declaration can give enforcers different values, and an invalid value can silently change a gate; refusing either condition keeps the owner in control instead of choosing an unrequested interpretation.\"\n-acceptance_criteria = \"\"\"A dial declared in both homes produces a refusal naming the key and both homes at the policy guard entry points; a wrong-typed or out-of-range dial is refused rather than defaulted.\"\"\"\n+rationale = \"A single declaration home gives the owner one place to inspect and change each dial. A duplicate declaration can give enforcers different values, and an invalid value can silently change a gate; refusing either condition keeps the owner in control instead of choosing an unrequested interpretation. The refusal crosses B-01 to reach the policy guard and B-04 to return its result to the caller.\"\n+acceptance_criteria = \"\"\"A dial declared in both homes produces a refusal naming the key and both homes at every guarded entry point; a wrong-typed or out-of-range dial is refused rather than defaulted.\"\"\"\n priority = \"S\"\n verification = \"Test\"\n status = \"Approved\"\ndiff --git a/docs/test/test-cases.toml b/docs/test/test-cases.toml\nindex 898ccd90b..abde096f6 100644\n--- a/docs/test/test-cases.toml\n+++ b/docs/test/test-cases.toml\n@@ -333,7 +333,7 @@ phase = 1\n [test.TC-031]\n verifies = [\"SR-031\", \"LLR-031\", \"IF-037\"]\n level = \"Integration\"\n-method = \"\"\"Drive each shipped commit and push hook and the checker with a policy file containing a dotted key, an inline table, and a multi-line string. For each form, compare every policy answer with the TOML parser's answer and assert no hook or checker refuses the file for its shape. Separately drive a privacy gate or reviewer declaration under another table and assert every reader, including the agent layer, reads gate on and reviewer required. Drive an unreadable declaration and a mixed declaration through each hook; run the policy suites to confirm every enforcer reads one value per dial. Exercise the unwired-reviewer posture: only the exact opt-down value under the policies table warns, a copied value requires review, and the legacy record answers only when that key is absent. Inspect that no hook reads a declared line itself.\"\"\"\n+method = \"\"\"Drive each shipped commit and push hook and the checker with a policy file containing a dotted key, an inline table, and a multi-line string. For each form, compare every policy answer with the TOML parser's answer and assert no hook or checker refuses the file for its shape. Separately drive a privacy gate declaration under another table and assert every reader, including the agent layer, reads gate on; drive a privacy reviewer declaration there and assert every reader reads reviewer required. Drive an unreadable declaration and a mixed declaration through each hook; run the policy suites to confirm every enforcer reads one value per dial. Exercise the unwired-reviewer posture: only the exact opt-down value under the policies table warns, a copied value requires review, and the legacy record answers only when that key is absent. Inspect that no hook reads a declared line itself.\"\"\"\n tier = \"Full\"\n expected = \"Satisfies SR-031 AcceptanceCriteria\"\n automated = \"Yes\"\n@@ -1513,11 +1513,11 @@ phase = 5\n [test.TC-152]\n verifies = [\"SR-138\", \"LLR-156\"]\n level = \"Integration\"\n-method = \"\"\"Run the migration cases in the process-config suite: a full conversion deletes every legacy file and lands all three approval dials; a second pass is a no-op; a hand-trimmed process.toml leaves the legacy file in place with its value intact; the F5 translation tables are pinned equal and the retired enum key is absent from the shipped template.\"\"\"\n+method = \"\"\"Run the migration and bootstrap writer cases: a full conversion deletes every legacy file and lands all three approval dials; a second pass is a no-op; a hand-trimmed process.toml leaves the legacy file in place with its value intact; an inline table, dotted key, and key-like line inside a multi-line string each refuse an unconfirmable in-place write without changing the file; a refused migration keeps its legacy file; the ordinal migration ignores a string decoy; and an ordinary policy file still migrates and accepts an in-place update. Assert that a scaffold flag reports refusal without a value it did not write, that the F5 translation tables are pinned equal, and that the retired enum key is absent from the shipped template.\"\"\"\n tier = \"Full\"\n expected = \"Satisfies SR-138 AcceptanceCriteria\"\n automated = \"Yes\"\n-evidence = \"tests/test_process_config.py;tests/test_rule_sync.py\"\n+evidence = \"tests/test_process_config.py::test_migration_folds_every_dial_in_and_deletes_the_file; tests/test_process_config.py::test_migration_never_deletes_a_file_it_could_not_write; tests/test_process_config.py::test_migration_leaves_an_unparseable_value_in_place; tests/test_rule_sync.py::test_the_leg